{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_641525144#1_1418394479","relevance":2,"reason":"This legal dictionary page is about the USA PATRIOT Act and gives related Senate support evidence for an earlier similar Senate bill, passed 96–1 with only Sen. Russell Feingold dissenting, but the snippet does not give the final Patriot Act House 357–66 and Senate 98–1 totals.","learned":"A legal dictionary source describes the legislative background and says a similar Senate bill passed 96–1 on Oct. 12, 2001 with only Russell Feingold dissenting."},"action":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_641525144#1_1418394479","reason":"Inspect the full legal dictionary document because it may continue from the preliminary Senate bill to the final USA PATRIOT Act passage and provide the exact final support totals."}}
{"request":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_641525144#1_1418394479"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_641525144#1_1418394479","document":"USA PATRIOT Act legal definition of USA PATRIOT Act ... USA PATRIOT Act of 2001 USA PATRIOT Act of 2001 USA PATRIOT Act of 2001 Further readings Cross-references Mentioned in ? Legal browser ? Full browser ?  ... Critics of the act have charged that the government gained the power to investigate and detain persons with little oversight from the courts. In the aftermath of the september 11, 2001, terrorist attacks, U.S. political leaders sought to address terrorism with new vigor. President george w. bush and Attorney General john ashcroft presented Congress with proposed legislation on September 17, 2001, that focused on intelligence gathering, immigration, criminal justice, and Money Laundering. The administration sought new powers to conduct searches of people suspected of terrorism; to detain and deport persons suspected of terrorist involvement; and to remove statutes of limitations on terrorism. In addition, the administration wanted the Justice Department to have the power to place wiretaps on the phones and computers of anyone suspected of terrorism. This initial proposal became the framework for the USA PATRIOT Act, which was first introduced in the House of Representatives on October 2. A similar law was introduced in the Senate on October 4, and on October 12 it passed on a vote of 96–1, with only Senator Russell Feingold (D-Wisc.) dissenting.","ok":true,"op":"document"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"28/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_641525144#1_1418394479","relevance":2,"reason":"The full legal dictionary document is about the USA PATRIOT Act and provides related legislative-support evidence for a preliminary Senate version, which passed 96–1 with only Sen. Russell Feingold dissenting, but it does not include the final USA PATRIOT Act passage totals of House 357–66 and Senate 98–1.","learned":"The Free Dictionary/legal dictionary entry confirms a preliminary Senate version of Patriot Act legislation passed 96–1 on Oct. 12, 2001 with only Russell Feingold dissenting, but lacks final-passage totals."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q808","reason":"The q808 wording around sole Senate opposition and House support has produced new sources; continue it to look for remaining unjudged documents that may include complete final Patriot Act support totals."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q808"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_641525144#2_1418396067","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q808","rank":10,"snippet":"\"headings\": \"USA PATRIOT Act of 2001 USA PATRIOT Act of 2001 USA PATRIOT Act of 2001 Further readings Cross-references Mentioned in ? Legal browser ? Full browser ? \" , \"segment\": \"and to remove statutes of limitations on terrorism. In addition, the administration wanted the Justice Department to have the power to place wiretaps on the phones and computers of anyone suspected of terrorism. This initial proposal became the framework for the USA <cover>PATRIOT Act, which was first introduced in the House of Representatives on October 2. A similar law was introduced in the Senate on October 4, and on October 12 it passed on a vote of 96–1, with only Senator Russell Feingold (</cover>D-Wisc.) dissenting. The House passed its version the next day on another lopsided vote, 337–79. The bills incorporated what the administration wanted, but it also gave the government the authority to conduct secret searches of a suspect's property. The two bodies resolved differences between their bills, and both houses passed the act on October 25. President Bush signed the bill into law on October 26. Because of objections about the scope of the authority given to ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_1778472344#2_2688091675","rank":7,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_1778472344#3_2688093350","rank":8,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_593034728#0_869314168","rank":9,"relevance":3}]}}
