{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"patriot act\" (+ \"passed overwhelmingly\" \"overwhelmingly passed\" \"overwhelmingly approved\") (+ \"357\" \"66\" \"98\" \"1\") (+ feingold senate house))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_05_312090717#8_353125213","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q817","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.<cover>1_doc_05_312090717#8_353125213\" , \"end_char\": 7899.000000 , \"headings\": \"Patriot Act provisions have expired: What happens now? Patriot Act provisions have expired: What happens now? Story highlights (CNN) — What counterterrorism tools does the U.S. lose? So those tools are now completely gone? Why is it such a long process? So could America be less safe? What are the facts on the expiring capabilities? \" , \"segment\": \"Rand Paul seizes political moment with NSA protest The House overwhelmingly passed </cover>a bill, the USA Freedom Act, that would make big changes to the first, but leave the latter two provisions intact. That bill would have the telephone companies hold Americans’ telephone metadata and require the government to get a specific warrant to seize any telephone metadata – and not on millions of people, but instead on specific individuals. So those tools are now completely gone? Not exactly. FBI and NSA officials are allowed to continue using Section 215 and the roving wiretap provision in investigations they began before the June 1 expiration date. Any new investigations "}}
